---
import BaseLayout from '~/components/BaseLayout.astro';
import DocsShell from '~/components/DocsShell.astro';
import CodeBlock from '~/components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import { SITE, VERSIONS } from '~/data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Python — leptris docs"
  description="The leptris Python package: libleptris behind an lxml-shaped API. fromstring, findall (full XPath 1.0), xpath with namespaces and variables, SAX streaming, C14N."
>
  <DocsShell
    crumb="Python"
    title="Python — the leptris package"
    lede={`The Python binding wraps libleptris via cffi and mirrors lxml: parse, query, and serialize code written against lxml's read path ports with an import change. Version ${VERSIONS.py} is the first Python release — its own semver, pinning libleptris ${VERSIONS.lib} via libleptris-version.txt.`}
  >
    <h2>Setup</h2>
    <p>
      The package publishes to PyPI as <code>leptris</code> (Python 3.9+),
      built and tested on Linux, macOS, and Windows via trusted publishing:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`pip install leptris`} />
    <p>
      If the native <code>libleptris</code> shared library is not on the
      loader path for your platform, build it from source (see
      <a href="/docs/getting-started">getting started</a>) and point
      <code>LEPTRIS_LIB_PATH</code> — which must name the library
      <em>file</em> — at it:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`cmake -B build -S . -DLEPTRIS_BUILD_SHARED=ON
cmake --build build --target leptris_shared
export LEPTRIS_LIB_PATH=$PWD/build/src/libleptris.dylib`} />

    <h2>Quick start — the lxml shape</h2>
    <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`from leptris import fromstring, tostring

root = fromstring("<library><book id='1' lang='en'>Ulysses</book></library>")

root.tag                    # "library"
root[0].get("id")           # "1"
root[0].attrib              # {"id": "1", "lang": "en"}
root[0].text                # "Ulysses"

root.xpath("count(//book)")             # 1.0
[b.text for b in root.findall("book")]  # ["Ulysses"]

tostring(root[0], encoding="unicode")   # "<book id='1' lang='en'>Ulysses</book>"`} />

    <h2>The surface</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <tr><th>member</th><th>notes</th></tr>
      <tr><td><code>fromstring</code> / <code>XML</code> / <code>parse</code> / <code>tostring</code> / <code>c14n</code></td><td class="note">module-level, lxml names; <code>parse</code> takes paths and file-likes (no URLs) and returns a <code>Document</code> context manager.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Element.tag</code> / <code>.text</code> / <code>.tail</code></td><td class="note">ElementTree model; <code>tag</code> uses <code>{'{uri}local'}</code> Clark notation; adjacent text and CDATA runs merge, as lxml&rsquo;s default parser does.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Element.attrib</code> / <code>.get</code> / <code>.keys</code> / <code>.items</code></td><td class="note"><code>attrib</code> is a read-only Mapping — the parser is read-only.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>elem[i]</code>, <code>len(elem)</code>, iteration, slices</td><td class="note">child indexing, never attribute lookup.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>getparent</code> / <code>getnext</code> / <code>getprevious</code></td><td class="note">navigation; sibling accessors are single FFI calls.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>iter</code> / <code>iterdescendants</code> / <code>itertext</code></td><td class="note">lxml iteration protocol.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>find</code> / <code>findall</code> / <code>findtext</code></td><td class="note">accepts <strong>full XPath 1.0</strong> — a superset of ElementPath — including <code>{'{uri}local'}</code> names.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>xpath(expr, namespaces=…, variables=…)</code></td><td class="note">W3C XPath 1.0; pre-bound namespace prefixes, and <code>variables</code> — a leptris extension.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>namespace</code> / <code>prefix</code> / <code>sourceline</code></td><td class="note">the element&rsquo;s own namespace binding, and its 1-based source line (lxml parity, since 1.5.0).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Document.getroot</code> / <code>write</code> / <code>parse_file</code> / <code>process_xinclude</code></td><td class="note">document lifecycle, file output, XInclude splice.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>leptris.sax</code></td><td class="note">one-shot SAX parse and a streaming push parser — constant memory, events emitted as chunks arrive.</td></tr>
    </table>

    <h3>Namespaces, variables, canonical XML, SAX</h3>
    <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`root.xpath("//x:item", namespaces={"x": "urn:ex"})
root.xpath("//book[@id=$id]", variables={"id": "2"})
c14n(root, exclusive=True)

from leptris import sax
sax.parse(xml, handler)                        # one-shot
with sax.StreamingParser(handler) as parser:   # push, constant memory
    parser.feed(chunk, final=last)`} />

    <h2>Migrating from lxml</h2>
    <p>
      Most read-path code needs only the import change. What is
      deliberately <strong>absent</strong>, and why:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tree building</strong> (<code>etree.Element</code>, <code>SubElement</code>, <code>append</code>, <code>set</code>) — this binding is read-only today (the core has partial mutation upstream — content setters, <code>set_root</code>, <code>append_child</code> — not surfaced yet); build trees elsewhere.</li>
      <li><strong><code>iterparse</code></strong> — use <code>leptris.sax.StreamingParser</code> (callback-based, constant memory).</li>
      <li><strong>Smart strings</strong> — XPath string and attribute results are plain <code>str</code>.</li>
      <li><strong><code>nsmap</code></strong> — use <code>namespace</code> / <code>prefix</code> and <code>xpath(namespaces=…)</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Compiled XPath objects, parser options</strong> — upstream asks: a compiled-expression C API, per-parse options.</li>
      <li><strong>Undeclared XPath prefixes</strong> evaluate to an empty nodeset instead of raising.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Memory model</h2>
    <Callout kind="warn">
      <p>
        <strong>The Document owns the tree and its pool.</strong> Accessor
        strings are copied into Python <code>str</code> at the boundary, so
        nothing you hold depends on document lifetime after a call returns.
        Elements keep a reference to their Document, so the pool cannot be
        freed while any wrapper is alive. Prefer explicit
        <code>close()</code> or the context manager; using an element after
        its document is closed raises <code>LeptrisError</code>.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2>Errors</h2>
    <p>
      <code>LeptrisError</code> is the base; <code>ParseError</code> and
      <code>XPathError</code> subclass it. Since 1.5.0, XPath errors prefer
      the <strong>document-scoped</strong>
      <code>leptris_document_last_error</code> — immune to concurrent
      operations on other documents. <code>leptris.libleptris_version()</code>
      reports the loaded library at runtime.
    </p>

    <h2>Benchmarks</h2>
    <p>
      The binding ships <code>benchmarks/matrix.py</code> — the Python twin
      of the Ruby matrix, same fixtures and operations against lxml,
      ElementTree, and minidom — and it runs in CI. The 1.4/1.5 line was
      tuned against it: engine-side subtree walks took traversal from 14×
      behind lxml to 2.6×, and batch nodesets halved plain queries. Current
      numbers and the honest losses:
      <a href="/benchmarks#python">the benchmarks page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Where next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={`${SITE.githubPy}`}>leptris-py</a> — source, tests, and the benchmark matrix.</li>
      <li><a href="/benchmarks">Benchmarks</a> — the per-language race.</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/internals">Internals &amp; FFI</a> — the ABI contract the cdef mirrors.</li>
    </ul>
  </DocsShell>
</BaseLayout>
